\section{Introduction}

Astrophysical $N$-body simulations traditionally suffer from secular energy
drift, non-reversible trajectories, and violations of Liouville's theorem due
to floating-point asymmetries and non-symplectic integration schemes. The
Temporal Symmetry Astrophysics Simulation Engine (TSSE) addresses these
limitations by enforcing exact time symmetry and symplectic structure at
billion-body scale. This white paper presents the mathematical foundation,
algorithmic architecture, and empirical verification of TSSE.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Hamiltonian Framework
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Hamiltonian Structure}

Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a $2n$-dimensional phase space with canonical coordinates
$(q,p)$ and symplectic form
\begin{equation}
  \omega = \sum_{i=1}^{n} dq_i \wedge dp_i.
\end{equation}

The Hamiltonian governing the system is separable:
\begin{equation}
  H(q,p) = T(p) + V(q),
\end{equation}
where $T(p)$ is the kinetic energy and $V(q)$ includes Newtonian gravitational
potential and post-Newtonian relativistic corrections.

The continuous flow $\Phi_t$ satisfies Hamilton's equations:
\begin{equation}
  \dot{q}_i = \frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i}, \qquad
  \dot{p}_i = -\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_i},
\end{equation}
and preserves $\omega$:
\begin{equation}
  \Phi_t^{*} \omega = \omega.
\end{equation}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Symplectic Integrator
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Symmetric Composition Integrator}

The TSSE employs a Yoshida-type symmetric composition integrator. The exact flow
over step $h$ is formally:
\begin{equation}
  \Phi_h = e^{h L_H} = e^{h (L_T + L_V)},
\end{equation}
where $L_T$ and $L_V$ are Liouville operators.

The numerical flow is constructed as:
\begin{equation}
  \Phi_h^{\text{num}} = \prod_{k=1}^{K} e^{a_k h L_T} e^{b_k h L_V},
\end{equation}
with coefficients chosen to ensure:
\begin{equation}
  \Phi_{-h}^{\text{num}} = (\Phi_h^{\text{num}})^{-1}.
\end{equation}

Each substep is a canonical transformation, guaranteeing symplecticity.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Time-Reversal Operator
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Time-Reversal Involution}

Define the involution:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{T}(q,p,t) = (q,-p,-t).
\end{equation}

Hamilton's equations are invariant under $\mathcal{T}$, and the discrete flow
satisfies:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{T} \circ \Phi_h^{\text{num}} = \Phi_{-h}^{\text{num}} \circ \mathcal{T}.
\end{equation}

This ensures exact temporal symmetry.

